Outline for getting randomly complex initial conditions into the WVT setup
**************************************************************************

In order to make this as useful as possible, we will do this in two
separate steps. In the first, we will take whatever grid we have into
a set of pseudo SPH particles. In the second step, we will then use
this set of particles and SPH interpolate between them to get the new
set of particles. This will allow us to have the same
setup for regridding an existing SPH simulation, which will be
extremely useful for restarting a simulation at a different
resolution.


Step I:
-------

Take whatever grid you have the initial conditions on (AMR,
cylindrical, doesn't really matter) and turn it into a set of SPH
particles at the grid centers. 

1) Turn initial conditions set into a set of pseudo SPH particles.

2) In case you haven't given the SPH particles sizes for the grid, try
to find h by settting a constant number of neighbors. For a cartesion
grid, h should simple be the grid spacing for example.



Step II:
--------

Use the existing set of SPH particles and interpolate between them to
get the new particle setup.

1) Load SPH data set

2) Do WVT iterations. wvt_hofpos should now be an input from the
interpolation. Note that the grid itself needs its proper smoothing
lengths, which are necessary for interpolation the SPH properties in a
later step. Instead, we another tag that is unused, and which we use
as the "target smoothing length" for WVT. wvt_hofpos and SPHofpos have
to take these two smoothing lengths into account.

3) Remove all SPH particles that are outside of the setup area,
i.e. that have no neighbors within the next grid particle's smoothing
length.




Open Issues: 
------------

- How do I handle empty space now, in particular the boundary, which
may be anything now. So far, this has been handled by invoking ghost
particles across the boundary. There are two principle approaches. 

The first idea would be to have an "outer" boundary further out than
the size of the computational domain. Then, I would need to put a (or
rather a few) static "layer" of SPH particles around the computational
domain. Now in principle, this may be the easiest way to do things. In
particular if the outer layers all share the same resolution. Then, I
can just make up a ideally packed grid within the outer boundary. Any
particles within the computational domain would then be removed. All
of the other ones are kept with positions fixed, so they are excluded
from the iterations to save time.

The second and a little cleaner, but more complex approach would be to
actually find the "boundary" of the simulation and to define it as a
function of the boundary particles. The criteria on what is a boundary
will be different for each setup. Then the problem would be to put
ghost particles perpendicular to this boundary and continue as usual.
